WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.668 --> 00:00:06.539
When a shark bites a human,
they never get the same taste,

00:00:06.606 --> 00:00:09.976
let's say, as they
would by biting a fish.

00:00:10.043 --> 00:00:14.180
So generally, they will
release us and swim away.

00:00:14.247 --> 00:00:17.183
These incidents were
totally different.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:20.587
The shark came in, attacked
the victim, and came back

00:00:20.653 --> 00:00:23.890
and attacked again
and again and again,

00:00:23.957 --> 00:00:27.026
and stayed with the
victims until they

00:00:27.093 --> 00:00:28.962
were removed from the water.

00:00:29.028 --> 00:00:31.231
That's a very determined animal.

00:00:31.297 --> 00:00:34.167
NARRATOR: So what could
cause sharks to display

00:00:34.234 --> 00:00:35.935
such brazen determination?

00:00:39.706 --> 00:00:44.144
For Collier, there can
be only one explanation.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:48.047
In my opinion, these animals
were not investigating.

00:00:48.114 --> 00:00:50.884
These were all
predatory attacks.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:54.888
These sharks were attempting
to feed on the victim.

00:00:54.954 --> 00:00:59.692
NARRATOR: Sharks trying
to feed on humans.

00:00:59.759 --> 00:01:03.897
It's a terrifying and virtually
unprecedented revelation.

00:01:03.963 --> 00:01:07.300
It's extremely unique
and very, very rare.

00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:10.303
And we had to find out why.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:11.704
NARRATOR: So what
could cause Egypt

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:13.940
sharks to suddenly
start attempting

00:01:14.007 --> 00:01:18.211
to actually eat beachgoers?

00:01:18.278 --> 00:01:21.548
For Dr. Dan Huber,
such unnatural behavior

00:01:21.614 --> 00:01:24.684
suggests that something
equally unnatural

00:01:24.751 --> 00:01:28.188
must be occurring in the
predator's environment.

00:01:28.254 --> 00:01:32.859
In fact, it could point to some
kind of human intervention.

00:01:32.926 --> 00:01:35.562
Animals can be trained to
exhibit certain behaviors

00:01:35.628 --> 00:01:37.163
in response to a stimulus.

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.299
And the reason why this
works is because the stimulus

00:01:39.365 --> 00:01:41.634
is associated with a reward.

00:01:41.701 --> 00:01:46.206
NARRATOR: This concept
is known as conditioning.

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:48.041
One of the primary
ways that this happens

00:01:48.107 --> 00:01:50.543
is if a shark gets
hand fed over and over

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:53.413
and over again, it's
learning to associate people

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:54.881
with a food reward.

00:01:54.948 --> 00:01:56.683
Which means that it's
going to exhibit feeding

00:01:56.749 --> 00:01:57.750
behaviors around people, even if
that food reward isn't present.

